<Base title="Method & sources · Your Body, Now" description="How the curves and copy on Your Body, Now were made, and where the science comes from.">
  <main class="doc">
    <a class="back" href="/">← Back to the clock</a>
    <h1>Method &amp; sources</h1>

    <h2>What the curves show</h2>
    <p>
      The four lines (alertness, core body temperature, cortisol and melatonin) are stylised shapes, drawn to show
      <em>when</em> each one rises and falls across a typical day. They are relative, not measured values, and assume an
      adult who sleeps roughly from 23:00 to 07:00.
    </p>

    <h2>Your clock is your own</h2>
    <p>
      Chronotype shifts these patterns. Early birds run an hour or two ahead, night owls an hour or two behind, and
      teenagers tend to run later still. Shift work, jet lag and light at night all move the clock too.
    </p>

    <h2>Not medical advice</h2>
    <p>
      This site explains general physiology. If you struggle with sleep or energy, talk to a doctor or a sleep
      specialist.
    </p>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <p>The full reference list is being compiled and will appear here before launch.</p>
  </main>
</Base>

